# Line endings — added 2026-07-30 (Opus audit finding L10)
#
# Why this file exists. The development machine has `core.autocrlf=true`, and until now
# there was no `.gitattributes`, so git checked every text file out with CRLF line endings.
# Prettier's default `endOfLine: "lf"` then reported those files as misformatted. The result
# was that `npm run format:check` failed on 23 files locally while passing in CI — CI runs
# on Linux and checks out LF — so the local check could not be trusted and `npm run format`
# would have rewritten 23 files as pure line-ending churn.
#
# The repository content was never the problem: git has always stored LF (verified
# 2026-07-30 — zero CR bytes in the stored blobs). Only the working-tree checkout differed.
#
# `eol=lf` makes the checkout match what is stored, on every platform and for every
# contributor, so the local and CI results agree. This also matters for `DR-BM-002`'s
# succession assumption: a future maintainer on Windows would have hit the same false
# failures with nothing to explain them.

# Normalise all text files to LF in the working tree as well as in the repository.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that must keep CRLF to work on Windows.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary files — never touch these. Listed explicitly rather than relying on git's
# auto-detection, because a corrupted .docx or .pdf caused by line-ending conversion is
# both easy to cause and hard to notice: this repository stores funder guideline PDFs and
# exported Word documents that are test inputs and deliverables.
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.doc binary
*.xlsx binary
*.pptx binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.zip binary
